Sick building syndrome
Social & Environmental
Clustered symptoms in occupants, Eye/nose/throat irritation, Symptom resolution outside building, Inadequate HVAC ventilation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-039
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, incapacitating headache and dizziness requiring medical certification and symptom relief.
Location: Private residence (patient returned home early from work)
Present at Visit: Patient, roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing severe, unrelenting headache, nausea, and dizziness that began abruptly at their workplace and rendered them unable to drive safely or function. Urgent assessment was required to rule out acute neurological events (e.g., carbon monoxide poisoning or stroke) and manage the severe symptoms prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every afternoon in that office my head starts throbbing, my eyes burn, and today I got so dizzy I thought I would pass out."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-month history of chronic, unexplained headaches, severe fatigue, and difficulty concentrating that occur exclusively while indoors at their workplace. Today, these symptoms culminated in an acute episode of nausea and episodic dizziness. Key symptoms include irritation of the eyes, nose, and throat without any identifiable infectious cause, and dry, itchy skin. The patient explicitly describes a clustered onset of non-specific symptoms among multiple occupants of the same building (several colleagues report identical issues). There is pronounced relief of all symptoms shortly after leaving the building, or during weekends and holidays. The patient works in a newly renovated, poorly ventilated building with sealed windows.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely incapacitated by severe headache and dizziness today
Social/Safety Concerns: Occupational health hazard, impaired ability to work, risk of driving while dizzy

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Rubbing temples, wearing sunglasses indoors (photophobia), fatigued appearance, but alert and oriented
Physical Exam: Neurological exam is entirely normal with no focal deficits, normal gait (though patient feels subjectively dizzy), and no cerebellar signs. Fundoscopy is normal. ENT exam reveals mild, bilateral conjunctival injection and dry nasal mucosa, but no purulence or severe swelling. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable. Diagnostic tetrad features are starkly present: clear clustered symptoms in building occupants, eye/nose/throat irritation, striking symptom resolution outside the building, and a high clinical suspicion of inadequate HVAC ventilation.
Environmental Observations: Patient's home is well-ventilated and clean. Symptoms have already begun to mildly improve since leaving the workplace 2 hours ago.
Medication Reconciliation: Took paracetamol and ibuprofen at work with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Sick building syndrome (Acute severe tension-type headache/vestibular irritation) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Carbon Monoxide Poisoning - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, 3-month pattern of symptoms solely confined to the workplace, the lack of severe acute deterioration of other colleagues to the point of unconsciousness, and normal SpO2 (though carboxyhemoglobin requires blood gas).
2. Primary Migraine - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of an aura, the clustered nature of symptoms among coworkers, and the strict geographical correlation with the office building rather than typical migraine triggers.
3. Viral Upper Respiratory Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of fever, the chronic recurrent nature of the symptoms over months, and the rapid resolution of symptoms upon leaving the building.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe, disabling neurological and mucosal symptoms (headache, dizziness, eye irritation) directly tied to a highly suspicious occupational environment, mimicking a serious acute neurological or toxicological event. The necessity to immediately clinically rule out life-threatening causes (like acute CO exposure or stroke) and to provide potent symptom relief for the incapacitating headache definitively justifies the urgent after-hours assessment. The objective findings of a normal neurological exam but visible mucosal irritation support the diagnosis of a severe environmental/occupational exposure syndrome.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administration of a strong oral analgesic/anti-inflammatory (e.g., Naproxen or Ketorolac) and an antiemetic (e.g., Metoclopramide) to break the acute headache and nausea cycle. Provision of a medical certificate for 2 days to ensure complete removal from the environmental trigger and allow physiological baseline reset.
Positive Behavioral Change: By reporting these clustered symptoms formally to your Occupational Health and Safety (OHS) representative tomorrow, you force a formal investigation of the building's air quality, which is the only permanent way to cure these daily headaches for you and your team.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Provision of a detailed medical letter outlining the suspected occupational link for the patient's employer/OHS department.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to review symptoms and organize basic blood panels to formally exclude systemic illness.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the headache suddenly becomes explosive or the "worst of your life," or if you develop unilateral weakness, slurred speech, or intractable vomiting.

